  "question": "If $\\vec{p}$ and $\\vec{q}$ are unit vectors such that $\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}$ is also a unit vector, find the angle between $\\vec{p}$ and $\\vec{q}$.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "Let $|\\vec{p}| = |\\vec{q}| = 1$ and $|\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}| = 1$.<br /><br />$|\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}|^2 = (\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}) \\cdot (\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}) = \\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{p} + 2\\vec{p}\\cdot\\vec{q} + \\vec{q} \\cdot \\vec{q}$.<br /><br />$= 1 + 2(\\vec{p}\\cdot\\vec{q}) + 1 = 2 + 2(\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q})$.<br /><br />But $|\\vec{p} + \\vec{q}|^2 = 1$.<br /><br />$1 = 2 + 2(\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q}) \\Rightarrow \\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$.<br /><br />Since $\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q} = |\\vec{p}||\\vec{q}|\\cos\\theta$, we get $\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$.<br /><br />Therefore $\\theta = 120^{\\circ}$.",
